  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14267656/assets/97a62e08b5c79f93122eecf4e90035419f65c6ad1faf612b0b676fd1c40abc49.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $g$ is part of the exponential curve $y={2}^{x-a}+b$ shown in the figure above. Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,b\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the horizontal asymptote of $y={2}^{x-a}+b$ is $y=b.$ \n\nIn the figure above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4.$ Hence, $b=4.$\n\nNext, we use the point $(5,6)$ to determine the value of $a.$\nSubstituting $(5,6)$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{5-a}+4 & =6 \\\\ {2}^{5-a} & =2 \\\\ 5-a & =1 \\\\ a & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4$ and $b=4.$",
